==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|extricatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|extricō}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɛks.tɹɪ.keɪt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-extricate.ogg|a=US}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[free]], [[disengage]], [[loosen]], or [[untangle]].
#: {{ux|en|I finally managed to '''extricate''' myself from the tight jacket.}}
#: {{ux|en|The firefighters had to use the [[jaws of life]] to '''extricate''' Monica from the car wreck.}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} To [[free]] from [[intricacy|intricacies]] or [[perplexity]].
#* '''1662:''' Thomas Salusbury, ''Galileo's Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems'' (Dialogue Two)
#*: ''Your argumentation ... is invelloped with certain intricacies, that are not easie to be '''extricated'''.''

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|extricable}}
* {{l|en|extrication}}
* {{l|en|inextricable}}

